"<strong>\"A heavier bob swings faster.\"</strong> Mass cancels.",
"<strong>\"A bigger swing takes longer because it travels farther.\"</strong> For small swings it also moves faster, and the two cancel; only large swings take noticeably longer.",
"<strong>\"Length is the string length.\"</strong> It is to the bob's center of mass (and for a rod, the effective length is 2L/{#}).",
"<strong>\"A pendulum's period never depends on its amplitude.\"</strong> That holds only for small swings. At {#}° the period is {#}% longer than {#}π√(L/g); at {#}° it is {#}% longer.",
"<strong>\"Chaos means random.\"</strong> The equations have no randomness: the same start always gives the same motion. Nearby starts separate exponentially, so any tiny uncertainty grows.",
"<strong>\"Better measurements fix chaos.\"</strong> The error grows like e^(λt), so each factor of {#} in starting accuracy adds only ln({#})/λ, about {#} s here.",
"<strong>\"A chaotic system is chaotic everywhere.\"</strong> At low energy the double pendulum is regular, and even at high energy the Poincaré section shows regular islands in the chaotic sea.",
